(() => {
const premium=[{id:"life-hacks",title:"Series of Life Hacks to Live By",author:"Taiwo Abdulgafar Adekunle",kind:"premium",description:"A reflective collection on perspective, stillness, discipline, time, love, marriage, boundaries, fear, failure, humility, and modern life.",themes:["Wisdom","Relationships","Discipline","Mindset","Purpose"],chapters:["Foundations of a Good Life","Matters of the Heart","Mindset and Motivation","Social Truth & Modern Struggles"],buy:"https://payhip.com/b/stSQH",read:"reader.html"}];
const free=[
{id:"meaningful-life",title:"The Architecture of a Meaningful Life",palette:["#315f52","#102b25"],themes:["Purpose","Wisdom","Self-awareness"],description:"Build a life from values rather than appearances: attention, choices, habits, work, relationships, and the quiet structures that help a person live with intention.",chapters:[
["The Foundation Beneath the Noise","A young builder admired a beautiful house until heavy rain exposed cracks in its foundation. He learned that what is hidden can determine the fate of everything visible.","A meaningful life rests on principles that remain useful when praise disappears: honesty, responsibility, curiosity, compassion, and the willingness to revise a mistaken belief. These foundations shape decisions when nobody is watching.","Write down three values you want your ordinary choices to express. For each, name one small behaviour you can practise this week."],
["Attention Is a Form of Wealth","A woman collected notebooks and plans for a future project. A neighbour began with one notebook and completed a first draft. The difference was not better tools; it was where each person placed attention.","Attention is finite. What repeatedly receives it begins to shape what feels important and possible. Purpose requires deliberate choices about what deserves the clearest part of the mind, without treating every distraction as a moral failure.","Notice one distraction that repeatedly interrupts you. Protect fifteen minutes for a task that matters and record what made it easier or harder."],
["Choose Principles Before Pressure Arrives","A market seller decided to describe her goods honestly even when customers could not easily check their quality. When a larger profit tempted her, her prior decision gave her a point of reference.","Values are most useful when considered before a difficult moment. A principle is not a rigid answer to every situation; it is a steady question that helps you examine options when convenience and fear compete.","Finish this sentence: Even when it costs me convenience, I want to be a person who… Choose one situation where you can practise that value."],
["Make Room for the Ordinary","A student kept postponing happiness until graduation, a better job, and a new city. Years later, a note reminded her that a peaceful walk home had once felt precious. Life had been happening while she waited.","Ambition gives direction, but the present becomes a waiting room when every moment is postponed. Ordinary life contains relationships, repetitions, and small acts of care from which much meaning is made.","Notice one ordinary thing that supports you: a conversation, a meal, a skill, or a safe place. Give it your full attention once this week."],
["Build Systems That Carry You","A gardener made watering part of her morning route instead of relying on memory. On busy days the routine carried her; on quiet days it still worked. The garden grew through repeated care.","Goals describe a destination; systems shape the steps repeated on the way. A useful system reduces friction and allows imperfect days. Structure should support your life, not turn every hour into a productivity contest.","Choose one goal and design a ten-minute routine. Decide when it happens, what tools you need, and how you will restart after missing a day."],
["Measure a Life by Its Effects","When an old teacher retired, students remembered a patient explanation, a fair correction, and encouragement at the right time. Few recalled classroom decorations. Her influence had travelled through small actions.","A life can be measured through care, useful work, harm repaired, knowledge shared, and people treated with dignity—not only status or recognition. These measures widen the picture of success without ignoring practical needs.","At week's end, ask what you contributed, learned, and need to repair. Choose one action that makes your presence more helpful without abandoning your own needs."]]},
{id:"loving-without-losing",title:"The Art of Loving Without Losing Yourself",palette:["#263e5b","#111e31"],themes:["Love","Boundaries","Relationships"],description:"A grounded exploration of affection, communication, trust, boundaries, and the difference between caring deeply and abandoning your identity.",chapters:[
["Love Is Attention, Not Possession","Two friends cared deeply, but one treated every separate plan as rejection. Their bond steadied when they learned that closeness did not require constant access.","Care is not ownership. Healthy affection makes room for dignity, friendships, responsibilities, and independent thoughts. Trust is not perfect certainty; it is communication and a willingness to respond to evidence rather than control every possibility.","In one relationship, write what you need in plain language. Invite the other person's needs too; focus on a conversation rather than winning a verdict."],
["Know the Shape of Your Own Life","A musician stopped practising because every free hour went to other people's emergencies. She protected two evenings for music and found she could help more freely when her own life had not disappeared.","Your interests, rest, values, and friendships are part of the person with whom others build a relationship. Self-respect is not proof of reduced affection; it helps closeness remain sustainable.","Protect one grounding commitment this week. If someone is affected, explain the boundary kindly and distinguish disappointing a person from doing something wrong."],
["Speak Before Resentment Speaks","A housemate silently took on every shared task, hoping the other would notice. A small forgotten chore finally triggered a large argument; the real issue was the unspoken need beneath it.","People cannot reliably respond to needs they have not heard. Describe specific observations, explain the impact, and make a concrete request. Avoid turning one event into a permanent judgment of character.","Try: When this happens, the impact is… Could we agree on this change? Practise the sentence before a conversation that matters."],
["Trust Is Built in Repetitions","One colleague made grand promises but often missed simple arrangements. Another promised less and followed through. Over time, the second person's reliability became more reassuring than dramatic declarations.","Trust grows through consistency, honesty about limits, accountability after mistakes, and respect for agreements. Nobody fulfils every promise perfectly; what matters is communicating early and changing behaviour when needed.","Choose one promise you made. Fulfil it, renegotiate it honestly, or apologise and state what you will do differently."],
["Boundaries Protect What Matters","A gatekeeper left a garden gate open so everyone felt welcome. Soon the garden was trampled. Agreed visiting times allowed people to come while giving the garden room to recover.","A boundary explains what you can offer, what you cannot accept, and how you will protect your well-being. It is not punishment. A clear boundary can be warm and firm at once.","Identify one recurring situation where you say yes but mean no. Prepare a respectful sentence and use it without a long defence."],
["Love Must Meet Reality","Someone stayed attached to a relationship's early promise and explained away repeated disrespect. A trusted friend asked her to consider not only what it might become, but what it repeatedly was.","Affection does not erase the importance of safety, respect, compatibility, and mutual effort. Decisions should consider patterns over time, not only potential or fear of being alone.","Look at a repeated pattern: what is improving, what repeats, and which conversations have led to action? If you feel unsafe, seek help from a trusted person."]]},
{id:"peace-within",title:"The Noise of the World and the Peace Within",palette:["#c09a4b","#74501e"],themes:["Mental peace","Perspective","Society"],description:"A reflective guide to comparison, public approval, appearances, digital noise, quiet growth, and protecting a clear inner life.",chapters:[
["The Crowd Is Not a Compass","A traveller followed the widest road because the crowd hurried there. It led away from her destination. A local map showed that the narrower road was the one she had intended to take.","Popularity tells us what many people choose, not what is right for every situation. Trends may offer useful ideas, but they cannot replace judgment about evidence, responsibilities, and values.","Before following a trend, write one reason it may help and one reason it may not fit your circumstances."],
["Comparison Hides Missing Chapters","A student compared her first draft with a published author's finished book and decided she lacked talent. She could not see the abandoned drafts and years of practice behind the result.","Comparison often places your private process beside another person's public outcome. Let achievements inspire learning, not become a verdict on your worth. Study the method, not just the display.","Choose one person you compare yourself with. Identify a skill to learn, then set a personal benchmark for your own next step."],
["Quiet Is Not Stagnation","A tree looked unchanged while its roots spread beneath the soil. A passer-by called it unproductive because no fruit was visible; the gardener understood that visible growth needed hidden work.","Learning, healing, preparation, and rebuilding may look slow from outside. Quiet progress still deserves honest evaluation, but should not be dismissed because it cannot be displayed.","Name one invisible form of progress you are making and one sign that it is developing, even if the final result is not ready."],
["Choose What Deserves a Reaction","A person answered every provocative message while trying to finish important work. Each reply brought another argument. The next day, a pause before non-urgent responses returned some control over the day.","Not every invitation to react deserves an immediate answer. A pause protects accuracy and dignity; it is not an excuse to avoid necessary responsibilities or conversations.","When provoked, pause and ask what outcome you want. Respond to the issue rather than attacking the person—or step away if no useful outcome is possible."],
["Curate the Voices You Hear","A gardener removed plants crowding the same patch of light. The remaining plants had room to grow. Caring for a space sometimes meant deciding what would not stay.","The voices and information we consume influence expectations and fears. Curating them is not avoiding disagreement; it is making room for accurate information, thoughtful challenge, rest, and sources that respect dignity.","Review the accounts and conversations occupying your attention. Keep what informs you, limit what repeatedly distresses without insight, and add one source that broadens your view."],
["Return to What Is Real","During a power cut, a family sat outside and talked. Their problems remained, but the conversation reminded them that life included more than screens and worries.","Peace does not require a perfect world. It can grow through a conversation, useful task, walk, meal, or quiet minutes without constant input. These moments restore perspective without solving everything.","Create a small daily ritual that brings you to the present. Notice how you feel before and after it."]]},
{id:"small-beginnings",title:"The Discipline of Small Beginnings",palette:["#6b4936","#2b1d15"],themes:["Discipline","Habits","Consistency"],description:"A practical-philosophical guide to beginning before confidence arrives, building habits that survive imperfect days, and keeping small promises.",chapters:[
["The First Step Is a Decision","A student wanted to write a book but spent weeks choosing notebooks. One morning she wrote a rough paragraph on scrap paper. The project became work she could improve instead of an idea she admired.","Beginnings expose the gap between imagination and skill. Waiting to feel ready can make that gap permanent. A small first action gives reality something to respond to: feedback, a result, or a reason to change direction.","Choose a delayed task and define the smallest useful version that can be completed in ten minutes. Do that before improving the plan."],
["Make the Right Action Easier","A man wanted to read each evening but left his book in another room. He placed it beside his chair, reducing the decisions between intention and action.","Environment, timing, reminders, and friction influence behaviour. A habit becomes easier when the tools are visible and the first step is clear. Designing surroundings is practical knowledge, not cheating.","Put the tool for one desired habit where you will use it. Remove one obstacle and attach the action to a routine you already perform."],
["Consistency Includes Returning","A runner missed several days because of rain and concluded the plan had failed. Her coach suggested a short walk instead of trying to recover every missed session at once.","Consistency is not a flawless record. It is returning after interruption without turning a missed day into a judgment about identity. A resilient routine includes a minimum version for difficult days.","Write the minimum version of one habit and decide how you will resume after a break. Aim for a reliable return, not punishment."],
["Use Feedback Without Shame","A craftsperson made a wobbly table. Instead of hiding it, she studied the legs and found her measurements were inconsistent. The error showed her what to practise.","Feedback is useful when it points to a change. Shame makes mistakes feel like proof you should stop; curiosity makes them information. Not all criticism is fair, so evaluate its source and substance.","Describe one mistake without insulting yourself, identify a factor you can influence, and choose one adjustment for next time."],
["Protect the Work From Performance","A young artist spent more time announcing projects than making them. She began practising privately before sharing, and the work gained room to develop.","Public accountability can help, but constant performance turns progress into a search for approval. Some work needs a protected stage where experimentation can be imperfect.","Give one project a short private work period before discussing it publicly. Measure learning or output, not applause."],
["Let Patience Work With Effort","A farmer prepared soil and cared for crops but could not pull seedlings upward to make them grow faster. His work mattered, and so did the season.","Patience is sustained effort without demanding instant results. Some goals depend on practice; others depend on conditions beyond control. A mature plan combines action with realistic timing.","Write what you can do this week and what you cannot force. Commit to the first without pretending to control the second."]]},
{id:"begin-again",title:"Courage to Begin Again",palette:["#7b3f38","#3b1d20"],themes:["Courage","Failure","Resilience"],description:"A humane guide to learning from disappointment, rebuilding confidence through evidence, and beginning again without denying that setbacks hurt.",chapters:[
["A Setback Is an Event","After a disappointing result, a learner called herself incapable. A mentor asked her to describe the result without turning it into a permanent label. They found two weak areas and a missing resource.","A setback carries information but cannot define an entire person or predict every future attempt. Separate the event, its conditions, and the interpretation attached to it. Admit responsibility where it belongs without claiming responsibility for everything.","Describe a disappointment: what happened, what contributed, and one adjustment available. Avoid 'always' and 'never' unless evidence truly supports them."],
["Grieve the Plan You Lost","A family tried to move on quickly after an important plan became impossible. Every new decision reminded them of what changed. Naming the disappointment allowed them to discuss alternatives honestly.","Beginning again does not require denying grief. A lost opportunity can matter even when other opportunities remain. Truthful feelings create room for decisions not ruled by panic or pretending.","Write what you hoped would happen and what you are learning to accept. Identify one value from the original plan that might be pursued another way."],
["Rebuild Confidence With Evidence","A speaker who stumbled during a presentation avoided speaking for months. A friend helped her practise a two-minute explanation with a small audience. Each attempt gave evidence that she could prepare and recover.","Confidence becomes durable when it grows from experience rather than slogans. Small completed actions provide evidence of capacity. Nervousness can coexist with preparation and useful action.","Choose a manageable version of a task you fear. Practise under low pressure, record what improved, and use it to choose the next step."],
["Ask for Help Without Surrendering Agency","A new worker struggled silently because she feared questions would prove she did not belong. A colleague explained one process, making her next questions more precise.","Independence is not doing everything alone. Advice, teaching, and support can reveal options that are difficult to see from inside a problem. Healthy help strengthens informed choices rather than removing responsibility.","Name the help that would move a problem forward—information, feedback, company, or practical assistance—and ask one trustworthy person a clear question."],
["Release the Perfect Explanation","A person waited to understand every reason a friendship ended before moving forward. Some questions received answers; others did not. He learned without solving every mystery about another person's choices.","Understanding matters, but complete explanation is not always available. You can decide what an experience taught you and what boundary you need without knowing every motive behind it.","List unanswered questions. Circle what you can investigate responsibly. For the rest, identify a choice you can make without full certainty."],
["Carry the Lesson, Not the Weight","A traveller carried a heavy stone from a difficult journey because putting it down felt like forgetting. A friend suggested keeping a small mark from it instead: a reminder without the full burden.","Learning from the past does not require living inside it indefinitely. Reflection is useful when it changes understanding or action; repeated self-punishment rarely adds new information. Let responsibility lead to repair where possible.","Name one lesson to keep and one burden you need not rehearse. If the experience feels overwhelming, speak with a trusted adult or qualified professional."]]},
{id:"wise-boundaries",title:"The Wisdom of Boundaries",palette:["#426d79","#183844"],themes:["Boundaries","Self-respect","Communication"],description:"An accessible exploration of personal limits, honest agreements, time, energy, family expectations, and the difference between kindness and unlimited availability.",chapters:[
["A Boundary Is a Clear Edge","Tools kept disappearing from a community garden because nobody knew how they could be borrowed. Simple rules about signing them out and returning them helped the garden remain welcoming and usable.","Boundaries describe a limit and a response. They do not control another person's feelings. They can concern time, privacy, money, space, or behaviour you will participate in; clarity makes respect observable.","Write a sentence stating what you can offer, what you cannot offer, and what you will do if the limit is not respected."],
["Kindness Needs a Sustainable Shape","A volunteer accepted every request and eventually resented the people she wanted to help. Two reliable shifts left her with more energy and a kinder spirit.","Kindness that depends on ignoring every limit becomes unsustainable. A respectful no can protect the quality of the yeses you choose. This is not abandoning responsibility; it is making promises you can honour.","Check your capacity before accepting a request. If you cannot take it on, decline clearly and offer a realistic alternative if possible."],
["Disappointment Is Not Proof of Wrongdoing","A student declined a last-minute request because an exam was next morning. Her friend felt disappointed. The student listened and acknowledged the inconvenience while keeping her decision.","A boundary may frustrate someone, but their feelings do not automatically determine your obligation. Fairness asks whether you communicated honestly and considered genuine commitments, not whether everyone was pleased.","Identify what you genuinely owe, what you do not owe, and how to communicate the difference respectfully."],
["Make Time Visible","A business owner agreed to meetings without checking his calendar, then missed deadlines. Treating the calendar as a record of real capacity helped him make honest commitments.","Time boundaries become easier when capacity is visible. A schedule makes trade-offs concrete; each promise uses time that cannot be promised elsewhere. This is about honesty, not optimising every minute.","Before accepting a commitment, check what would need to move. If nothing can move without harm, the answer may be no or not now."],
["Privacy Is Not Dishonesty","A young adult explained every personal decision to relatives, inviting arguments and feeling exposed. She began sharing what was necessary while keeping some plans private until ready.","Honesty does not require unlimited disclosure. People can be truthful while maintaining privacy about thoughts, plans, finances, and history. Privacy should not evade obligations, but closeness should not demand access to everything.","Prepare a calm response: 'I understand you're curious, but I'm keeping that private for now.' Repeat it without escalating if necessary."],
["Review Agreements as Life Changes","A family agreement made when everyone lived together stopped working after two members began new jobs. Revising shared responsibilities made the agreement useful again.","Boundaries can adapt as responsibilities, resources, and needs change. Review agreements openly rather than expecting others to notice. Flexibility is strongest when chosen honestly, not extracted through guilt.","Review one strained agreement. Ask what changed, what purpose still matters, and what new arrangement would be fair."]]},
{id:"quiet-becoming",title:"The Quiet Work of Becoming",palette:["#736b91","#302946"],themes:["Identity","Growth","Patience"],description:"A reflective book about identity as an ongoing practice, the dignity of learning, changing one's mind, and allowing private growth to precede recognition.",chapters:[
["You Are More Than This Chapter","An apprentice called himself a failure because he was still learning while others were earning. His instructor reminded him that one chapter is not a summary of the whole book.","People confuse their present condition with permanent identity. A difficult season or unfinished skill is real, but it does not tell the whole story. Useful identity leaves room for learning, accountability, and change.","Replace one fixed self-description with a precise description of the current challenge and the skill you need to practise."],
["Let Curiosity Interrupt Certainty","A mechanic insisted a machine had a familiar fault. A younger colleague asked about a new sound; testing revealed a different problem. Experience helped, but curiosity prevented a mistake.","Certainty can protect an outdated explanation. Curiosity makes room for evidence that complicates a favourite belief. Changing your mind after learning is not weakness; it shows that thinking remains responsive to reality.","Choose a strong opinion. Ask what evidence would make you reconsider it, then seek a serious source that treats the issue fairly."],
["Learn Without Performing Expertise","A beginner avoided questions to seem naturally talented. Her mistakes became harder to correct. Once she admitted what she did not understand, colleagues could help sooner.","Pretending to know may preserve an image briefly while delaying competence. Admitting uncertainty gives learning a starting point. Humility means accurately recognising what you know and what still needs work.","Ask one genuine clarifying question this week. Note how the answer changed your understanding or next action."],
["Protect a Private Practice","A dancer practised a difficult movement in an empty room long before performing it publicly. Private repetitions made the later performance possible even without applause.","Recognition can encourage, but it cannot substitute for practice. Private work allows experimentation without making every attempt a public statement about worth. Some work matters before anyone sees it.","Set a regular short session for a skill. Measure participation and learning rather than likes or immediate results."],
["Allow Values to Mature","A person once believed success meant never asking for help. Leading a team taught him that early questions prevented larger mistakes. He developed a more useful understanding of independence.","Experience may reveal limits in beliefs that once seemed complete. Values can become more precise as consequences become clearer. A mature principle sometimes becomes more balanced rather than louder.","Review a long-held belief. What strengthened it, what complicated it, and how would you revise it to include what you now understand?"],
["Become Through Repetition","A potter shaped clay each morning. Some pieces collapsed before firing, but repeated contact between intention, material, error, and adjustment taught her skill.","Becoming is often quiet: keeping a promise, apologising, practising, listening, and beginning again. Repetition does not guarantee perfection, but it creates conditions for reliable improvement.","Choose a quality you want to embody and define a behaviour that expresses it. Repeat it in a realistic setting, then review what helped."]]},
{id:"money-meaning",title:"Money, Meaning, and Enough",palette:["#58724d","#24341e"],themes:["Money","Values","Responsibility"],description:"A balanced reflection on money as a practical resource, pressure to display success, thoughtful spending, financial responsibility, and defining enough.",chapters:[
["Money Is a Tool, Not a Biography","Two neighbours earned different incomes. One spent to appear successful and worried about keeping up; the other planned around family needs and a modest goal. Income alone could not tell their full stories.","Money affects shelter, food, education, health, choice, and security. Yet it cannot describe a person's entire worth. Financial decisions become clearer when money is directed toward real priorities instead of used as a score ranking people.","List your priorities: essentials, obligations, savings or resilience, and discretionary wants. Adapt the order to your circumstances, not another person's lifestyle."],
["Distinguish Need From Display","A worker bought an expensive item after seeing friends praise similar purchases online. The excitement faded into worry about the bill. Waiting a day before buying helped her question the motive.","Spending can meet a need, express a value, bring pleasure, or seek approval. The question is not whether every purchase must be practical; it is whether the cost fits your resources and the value you expect.","Before a non-essential purchase, ask whether you want the item, the feeling, or the image it creates. If uncertain, pause and revisit later."],
["Build a Small Margin","A household that spent every available amount found an unexpected repair disruptive. They began setting aside a small amount when possible. The reserve did not remove uncertainty, but it created options.","A financial margin is not a guarantee against hardship; it can reduce decisions made under immediate pressure. Methods depend on income and obligations. Tracking patterns can help even before saving is possible.","If circumstances allow, choose a modest amount to reserve regularly. If not, track one week of spending to learn where money goes without judging yourself."],
["Talk About Money Clearly","Two relatives shared a cost but never discussed the exact amount or date. Each assumed the other understood, and resentment grew. Clear numbers and timing prevented similar confusion.","Money carries emotion, status, and obligation. Clear agreements reduce the risk that affection becomes mixed with unspoken expectations. Discuss amounts, deadlines, responsibilities, and what happens if circumstances change.","For a shared expense, write the amount, who is responsible, due date, and how changes will be communicated. Ask before committing."],
["Define Enough for This Season","A business owner kept expanding because every milestone quickly felt ordinary. She wrote what the business was meant to support: stable work, family time, and room to create. That list clarified which opportunities fit.","Enough is not identical for everyone and may change across seasons. Defining it does not abandon ambition; it gives ambition a purpose and helps distinguish useful growth from an endless race.","Write what financial progress should make possible in this season. Use it as one factor when evaluating a purchase or opportunity."],
["Let Success Include Responsibility","After receiving a raise, a young professional reviewed obligations, savings, and enjoyment. The money felt more satisfying because it had a purpose before it disappeared.","Responsible financial living includes planning, honest commitments, enjoyment, and seeking reliable information. It is not a moral ranking of people with different incomes; circumstances vary widely.","Review one financial decision: purpose, likely cost, alternatives, and effects on obligations. For complex decisions, consult a trustworthy qualified source."]]},
{id:"friendship-truth",title:"Friendship, Loyalty, and the Courage of Truth",palette:["#8a5d47","#40291f"],themes:["Friendship","Loyalty","Honesty"],description:"A study of reliable friendship, loyalty without blind agreement, difficult conversations, repairing trust, and knowing when distance is wiser than pretending.",chapters:[
["Presence Is More Than Proximity","Two neighbours lived close but rarely spoke beyond greetings. Another friend lived far away and remembered important dates, asked thoughtful questions, and made time for honest conversations.","Friendship requires attention, reliability, respect, and freedom to be honest without humiliation. Different friendships offer different support; no single person must meet every emotional or practical need.","Reach out with a sincere question about someone's life and listen without immediately turning the conversation toward yourself."],
["Loyalty Is Not Blind Agreement","A friend was about to make a decision that could harm a shared project. Others stayed silent to appear supportive. One person raised the concern privately and offered to examine options together.","Loyalty is not the duty to approve every choice. Sometimes it means telling the truth with care. Specific observations and genuine concern help more than humiliation or attacks on character.","If you disagree, describe the behaviour or risk rather than attacking character. Ask whether the person is open to hearing your concern."],
["Repair Requires More Than Sorry","A teammate repeatedly arrived late and apologised. The apologies lost meaning because the pattern never changed. When he acknowledged the effect and adjusted his plan, the apology gained evidence.","An apology acknowledges harm, but repair usually requires listening, accountability, and changed behaviour. The harmed person does not owe instant forgiveness; trust returns at a pace supported by actions.","Name what you did, acknowledge its impact, avoid excuses, and state what you will change. Follow through without demanding praise."],
["Let People Be Different","Two friends treated different hobbies as a test of loyalty. Later they allowed separate interests and found more to share because neither had to pretend to enjoy everything.","Friendship does not require identical tastes, beliefs, or schedules. Respectful differences can broaden understanding. Some differences affect compatibility, but ordinary variation is not automatically rejection.","Notice one harmless difference you have tried to change in a friend. Practise allowing it to remain different."],
["Choose Trust With Open Eyes","A person shared private information with a new acquaintance because the conversation felt warm. Later it was repeated elsewhere. She learned to let trust grow through observed discretion.","Openness is valuable, but trust is strengthened by evidence over time. Notice whether people keep confidences, respect boundaries, and behave consistently. Caution can be thoughtful without becoming cynicism.","Share gradually in a new friendship and observe how the person handles ordinary boundaries. Let trust grow as reliability becomes visible."],
["Distance Can Be Honest","A long friendship changed as both people developed new priorities. They stopped forcing the old routine and spoke honestly about the distance. They could value the past without pretending the present was unchanged.","Not every friendship lasts in the same form. Distance may arise from changing circumstances, unresolved harm, or incompatible needs. A change in contact should be thoughtful, not used as punishment.","Consider whether a conversation, new expectations, or distance would help a strained friendship. Communicate kindly rather than disappearing to avoid discomfort."]]},
{id:"questions-of-life",title:"The Questions We Ask of Life",palette:["#4c657d","#1e2c42"],themes:["Philosophy","Meaning","Judgment"],description:"A gentle exploration of the questions beneath daily decisions: what matters, what is true, what we owe one another, and how to live wisely with uncertainty.",chapters:[
["Ask Better Questions","A team spent days asking why a project failed, repeating blame. Someone asked what they would change if they tried again next week. The new question turned attention toward evidence and action.","Questions guide attention. Some trap us in accusation; others help separate facts, values, choices, and uncertainties. A better question does not guarantee an easy answer, but it creates a more useful space for honest thought.","Take a worry and write three questions: one about facts, one about values, and one about the next action. Notice which creates a clearer path."],
["Separate Fact From Interpretation","Two neighbours heard the same brief comment. One assumed insult; the other wondered whether the speaker was distracted. Asking for clarification revealed a smaller misunderstanding than the story around it.","Interpretation is unavoidable, but not identical to fact. When evidence is incomplete, the mind fills gaps with fear or habit. A wise response acknowledges the first interpretation while remaining open to others.","Write what you directly observed and what you inferred. Ask what information could confirm or challenge the inference before acting."],
["Live With Honest Uncertainty","A traveller wanted a complete map before a long journey. A guide explained that some roads had changed; the map could only show the best available information. Preparation and a willingness to revise became more useful than waiting.","Some decisions must be made with incomplete information. Good judgment identifies what is known, what remains unknown, what risks are acceptable, and what evidence should change the plan.","List the facts, uncertainties, and cost of waiting. Choose a responsible next step and decide what evidence would make you reassess."],
["Consider the Other View","A manager thought an employee lacked commitment after a missed deadline. Conversation revealed changing instructions and a missing resource. The deadline still mattered, but the solution also required better coordination.","Empathy is not automatic agreement. It is the effort to understand another person's situation before deciding what it means. Context can make judgment fairer without removing the need for boundaries.","Summarise the other person's view in a way they would recognise before explaining yours. Ask what information you may be missing."],
["Use Wisdom in Small Decisions","A person imagined wisdom as one grand insight. Later she noticed her most useful decisions were small: asking before assuming, admitting a mistake, checking a fact, and refusing an impossible promise.","Wisdom is not merely knowing impressive ideas. It is applying understanding with proportion, humility, and attention to consequences. Small decisions build habits of honesty, patience, and fairness.","Pause over one ordinary decision and ask: Is it true? Is it fair? What might it cause? What am I overlooking?"],
["Make Meaning Through Action","A retired librarian spoke not of one grand achievement, but of helping children find books, encouraging colleagues, and learning to listen. Meaning had arrived through many small acts.","People find meaning through faith, relationships, service, creativity, learning, or commitments to care. There is no single formula; what matters is giving values a place in actual decisions and remaining open to growth.","Answer: What do I want my ordinary choices to stand for? Choose one action this week that makes the answer visible."]]},
{"id":"honest-conversations","title":"The Courage of Honest Conversations","palette":["#376a63","#173a35"],"themes":["Communication","Honesty","Relationships"],"description":"A practical reflection on speaking clearly, listening without defensiveness, asking better questions, and repairing misunderstandings before silence becomes distance.","chapters":[["The Cost of Unspoken Words","A team kept missing deadlines because everyone noticed the same problem but assumed someone else would raise it.","Silence can feel safe, yet it leaves others guessing. Respectful honesty names an issue without attacking a person's character.","What important point have you avoided saying? Write one calm opening sentence."],["Listen to Understand","Two neighbours argued over a fence until one repeated the other's concern accurately. The disagreement became easier to solve once both felt heard.","Listening is not waiting for your turn. It means checking what you heard and remaining willing to update your understanding.","Summarise someone's view fairly before offering your own."],["Choose the Right Moment","A difficult topic raised in a crowded hallway became a public contest. The same conversation went better in a quiet, agreed setting.","Timing cannot guarantee agreement, but privacy, calm, and a clear purpose can reduce defensiveness.","Choose a suitable time and place for a conversation that matters."],["Ask Instead of Assuming","A delayed reply looked like rejection until a direct question revealed a family emergency.","Assumptions turn missing information into confident stories. Questions allow facts to replace guesswork.","What question could test an assumption you have made?"],["Disagree Without Disrespect","A committee found a stronger plan when members challenged ideas while continuing to treat one another as colleagues.","Disagreement can be useful when it stays focused on evidence and consequences rather than humiliation.","Explain one disagreement without labelling the person."],["Repair What You Can","After a careless remark, a friend apologised specifically, listened to the impact, and changed how they handled similar moments.","Repair includes acknowledging the effect, taking responsibility, and making a realistic change.","Identify your part in a misunderstanding and one action that could help repair it."]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"beyond-appearances","title":"A Life Beyond Appearances","palette":["#7b6545","#3e3020"],"themes":["Authenticity","Social pressure","Values"],"description":"A thoughtful guide to public image, comparison, private values, and building a life that remains meaningful when nobody is watching.","chapters":[["The Display Is Not the Whole Story","A visitor admired a shop window but never saw the careful workroom where the goods were made.","Visible success shows only a portion of reality. A fair judgment makes room for unseen effort, constraints, and costs.","What public image have you mistaken for the whole story?"],["Know What You Are Trying to Prove","A young professional bought things she did not need because she wanted to appear established. She felt freer when she named the insecurity behind the spending.","When choices are organised around proving worth, the audience becomes more important than the life being built.","Before a purchase or post, ask what need you hope it will meet."],["Private Values, Public Choices","A shopkeeper stayed honest in small transactions even when no customer could check the change.","Authenticity is consistency between professed values and ordinary conduct, not a perfect image or the absence of mistakes.","Choose one value and name a private action that would express it."],["Comparison Has a Price","A runner abandoned a healthy pace to match a stranger and lost the rhythm that had been working.","Other people's milestones may inspire, but their circumstances are not a complete measuring tool for your progress.","Set one benchmark that reflects your own needs and responsibilities."],["Let Success Have More Than One Shape","A family celebrated a relative who cared for others and learned a craft, not only the person with the highest title.","Status is one measure, but meaning may also include health, useful work, learning, relationships, and contribution.","List three forms of success you value beyond public recognition."],["Be Seen Without Performing","A friend shared an honest account of a difficult lesson without pretending to have mastered everything.","Being genuine does not require revealing everything. It means choosing what to share without pretending to be someone else.","Where could you be more honest while still protecting your privacy?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"practice-of-patience","title":"The Practice of Patience","palette":["#a67a42","#54351e"],"themes":["Patience","Growth","Perspective"],"description":"A calm exploration of waiting, effort, uncertainty, and knowing when patience means steady work—and when it means changing course.","chapters":[["Growth Has Its Own Pace","A gardener checked a seed every morning and worried when no shoot appeared. The work was happening before it became visible.","Not every meaningful process offers immediate evidence. Patience allows time for development while still checking whether conditions are right.","What are you expecting to grow faster than its process allows?"],["Waiting Is Not Always Idleness","A craftsperson studied measurements before cutting expensive material. Preparation saved more time than rushing would have.","Patient action can include research, practice, rest, and careful timing. Waiting is useful when it serves a clear purpose.","Name one preparatory action that would make your next attempt wiser."],["Distinguish Delay From Direction","A traveller waited through a storm, then changed routes when the bridge remained closed.","Patience should respond to evidence. Endurance is not refusing to reconsider a plan.","What evidence would tell you it is time to adjust your approach?"],["Do Not Rush Every Feeling","A heated message was drafted, saved, and reviewed the next morning. The final reply addressed the issue without escalating it.","Time can create space between emotion and action. A pause helps you respond according to your values.","Choose one situation where a brief pause could improve your response."],["Keep Effort Proportionate","A student studied consistently but also rested and asked for help instead of treating exhaustion as proof of commitment.","Patience includes sustainable effort. Constant strain can make a long goal harder to reach, not more deserving.","How can you make your effort more sustainable this week?"],["Recognise the Right Moment","A musician practised patiently and eventually performed when prepared, rather than waiting for a perfect feeling.","Patience is not endless postponement. It joins preparation with the courage to act when the next reasonable step is available.","What is ready enough to begin now?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"clear-thinking","title":"The Craft of Clear Thinking","palette":["#496b8b","#1d3147"],"themes":["Judgment","Evidence","Reasoning"],"description":"A practical guide to separating facts from assumptions, checking evidence, weighing alternatives, and making decisions with intellectual humility.","chapters":[["Name the Actual Question","A group debated which tool was best without agreeing on the task they needed it to perform.","Clear thinking begins by defining the problem. A vague question invites arguments that cannot settle anything.","Rewrite one confusing problem as a specific question."],["Facts and Interpretations","A short message sounded rude to one reader and hurried to another; asking the sender clarified the intention.","Interpretations matter, but they should not be confused with direct evidence. Keep the distinction visible.","Write one fact and one interpretation about a recent event."],["Look for Missing Evidence","A buyer relied on one glowing review until comparing several independent experiences.","A claim becomes stronger when it survives relevant evidence, not merely when it matches what we hoped to hear.","What source or fact would help you test a belief?"],["Consider More Than Two Options","A team thought it had to choose between rushing a project and cancelling it. A smaller pilot became a third option.","False choices hide workable alternatives. Generate options before committing to the first two presented.","List three possible responses to a decision you face."],["Update Without Shame","A researcher changed her conclusion when new measurements contradicted the first result.","Changing a belief in response to good evidence is a strength. Pride becomes costly when protecting an old view matters more than accuracy.","What evidence would make you revise a current opinion?"],["Decide and Review","A shop owner chose a trial period for a new service and set a date to review the results.","Some decisions cannot be made with certainty. A clear decision includes the reason, the risk, and the evidence that will trigger a review.","Set a review date and one success measure for a decision."]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"everyday-work","title":"The Meaning of Everyday Work","palette":["#7a5947","#3e2a20"],"themes":["Work","Craft","Contribution"],"description":"A reflection on dignity in ordinary work, skill, service, fair effort, and creating value without allowing a job title to define an entire life.","chapters":[["Work Is More Than a Title","A cleaner noticed a safety problem and prevented an accident while senior staff were distracted by a presentation.","A role's public status does not fully measure its usefulness. Every honest contribution deserves fair treatment and recognition.","Name one form of useful work that is often overlooked."],["Practise the Craft","A baker improved one part of a recipe each week instead of waiting for a perfect day to transform the whole business.","Skill grows through attention, feedback, repetition, and adjustment. Small improvements compound when examined honestly.","Choose one task and identify a specific skill to practise."],["Reliability Builds Trust","A colleague communicated early when a deadline became unrealistic and offered a revised plan.","Reliability is not pretending every task is easy. It means setting honest expectations and speaking up before others are left guessing.","What expectation should you clarify before it becomes a problem?"],["Protect Dignity at Work","A supervisor corrected an error privately and explained the standard instead of mocking the worker.","Good work culture treats people with dignity while keeping standards clear. Respect and accountability can coexist.","How could you give or request feedback more respectfully?"],["Rest Supports Good Work","A team made fewer mistakes after planning breaks during a demanding project.","Rest is part of sustainable effort. Constant availability can hide fatigue until quality and judgment decline.","What reasonable recovery habit would support your work?"],["Let Work Serve a Life","A person built a successful business but began reserving time for family, learning, and community service.","Work matters, but it is one part of a life. Achievement should support rather than consume other values.","What part of life needs deliberate space alongside work?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"forgiveness-and-repair","title":"A Gentle Guide to Forgiveness","palette":["#71826a","#344532"],"themes":["Forgiveness","Accountability","Healing"],"description":"A careful exploration of resentment, apology, accountability, changed behaviour, and the difference between forgiveness and restoring access or trust.","chapters":[["Name What Happened","A person tried to move past a broken promise without admitting how much it had affected their plans.","Honest repair begins with recognising the event and its impact. Naming harm is not the same as remaining trapped in it.","Describe what happened without exaggeration or minimisation."],["An Apology Must Be Specific","A friend stopped saying only 'sorry' and named the choice they regretted and the change they would make.","A meaningful apology acknowledges responsibility, listens to impact, and avoids making the injured person manage the apologiser's discomfort.","What would a specific apology include in a situation you know?"],["Forgiveness Is Not Forgetting","A neighbour released some bitterness but still agreed on clear arrangements after repeated missed commitments.","Forgiveness, trust, and access are different. A person can move forward while still using evidence and boundaries.","Which boundary would remain wise even if you chose to forgive?"],["Give Change Time to Become Visible","A relative made one generous gesture after years of unreliability. The family waited to see whether the pattern changed.","Promises matter less than repeated behaviour. Rebuilding trust is a process, not a demand for immediate reassurance.","What consistent actions would demonstrate genuine change?"],["Release the Need to Control the Past","A woman could not make another person understand her perspective, but she could choose how to spend her attention now.","You cannot rewrite an event or control another person's response. You can seek support, make decisions, and choose what deserves your energy today.","What part is outside your control, and what choice remains yours?"],["Repair Where It Is Safe and Possible","Two colleagues rebuilt cooperation through clear agreements and gradual follow-through.","Reconciliation requires willingness and reasonable safety from everyone involved. Distance can be appropriate when harmful patterns continue.","What would a safe, realistic next step look like—or would distance be wiser?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"wisdom-of-time","title":"The Wisdom of Time","palette":["#6d7e91","#273848"],"themes":["Time","Attention","Priorities"],"description":"A guide to choosing priorities, respecting limits, avoiding false urgency, and giving time to what genuinely matters.","chapters":[["Time Reveals Priorities","A calendar showed what a person repeatedly made room for more clearly than their intentions did.","Priorities become visible in patterns, though responsibilities and constraints matter too. A calendar is information, not a verdict on worth.","Review one week and notice where your time actually went."],["Urgent Is Not Always Important","A worker answered every notification while a significant task waited untouched.","Urgency is often loud. Importance may require protected attention and a decision not to respond immediately to everything.","Choose one important task to protect from interruptions."],["Leave Room for the Unexpected","A planner filled every hour until one delayed bus ruined the entire day.","A realistic plan includes buffers. Flexibility recognises that life contains uncertainty.","Add one small buffer to a plan this week."],["Say No With Clarity","A volunteer accepted every request and began missing the commitments already made.","A thoughtful no can protect a more important yes. Limits help people make promises they can honour.","Write a respectful decline for a request you cannot reasonably take on."],["Give People Your Attention","A family conversation improved when phones were put aside for a short, agreed period.","Attention is part of how we show respect. Small periods of genuine presence can matter more than distracted hours together.","Choose one conversation to give your full attention."],["Review Before You Repeat","A monthly review revealed that a recurring meeting no longer served its original purpose.","Time improves when habits and commitments are reviewed, not merely repeated. Keep what works and revise what does not.","What recurring activity deserves a fresh look?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"friendship-changes","title":"When Friendship Changes","palette":["#986b60","#4a2c2b"],"themes":["Friendship","Change","Trust"],"description":"A compassionate guide to changing seasons in friendship, honest expectations, loyalty, distance, and letting relationships evolve without bitterness.","chapters":[["People Grow in Different Directions","Two friends who once shared every interest found new responsibilities and schedules. Their friendship needed a different rhythm.","Change does not automatically mean betrayal. Relationships sometimes need new forms to remain meaningful.","What expectation about a friendship may need updating?"],["Effort Should Not Be Invisible","One friend always arranged meetings until they paused and asked whether the other could take the next turn.","Mutual effort need not be equal every week, but long-term imbalance deserves an honest conversation.","Name one practical way both people could contribute."],["Ask Before You Conclude","A missed celebration felt deliberate until the friend explained a private family difficulty.","A hurt feeling is real, but the first explanation may be incomplete. Ask for context before deciding what the event means.","What question could help you understand a disappointing moment?"],["Loyalty Includes Truth","A friend gently challenged a harmful decision rather than agreeing merely to avoid tension.","Loyalty is not automatic approval. Honest care can include disagreement offered with respect and concern.","How can you express a concern without trying to control the other person?"],["Allow Room for Distance","Two former close friends remained respectful after their daily lives no longer fit together.","Not every relationship must return to its earlier form. Distance can be handled without gossip, punishment, or pretending nothing changed.","What respectful boundary could make a changed friendship easier?"],["Appreciate What Was Real","An old photograph reminded a person that a friendship had mattered even though it was no longer close.","An ending or change does not erase what was valuable. Gratitude can coexist with grief and new boundaries.","What did a past friendship teach you that you want to carry forward?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"courage-of-ordinary","title":"The Courage to Be Ordinary","palette":["#6c7561","#2e382b"],"themes":["Self-worth","Simplicity","Contentment"],"description":"A thoughtful challenge to constant performance: finding dignity in ordinary days, quiet contribution, and a life not organised around applause.","chapters":[["You Do Not Need to Impress Everyone","A student stopped choosing activities solely for praise and rediscovered an interest she enjoyed learning.","Living for approval makes every audience a judge. A grounded life asks which choices fit your values and responsibilities.","Which activity would you choose even if nobody applauded?"],["Ordinary Days Are Real Life","A person waited for a major milestone to feel satisfied and overlooked the people and routines supporting them now.","Life is largely made of repeated ordinary moments. Recognising them does not mean abandoning ambition; it means being present while pursuing it.","Notice one ordinary thing that deserves appreciation today."],["Make Peace With Being a Beginner","A new cook avoided trying recipes because mistakes might be visible. Practice made the kitchen less intimidating.","Beginners need room to learn. Skill develops through imperfect attempts, feedback, and willingness to continue.","What could you practise without demanding immediate excellence?"],["Choose Enough for Yourself","A family kept upgrading possessions to match neighbours until they defined what genuinely served their needs.","Enough is personal and can change with circumstances. Defining it helps distinguish real needs from pressure to keep up.","What does 'enough' mean for one area of your life right now?"],["Let Contribution Be Quiet","A neighbour regularly checked on older residents without announcing each act.","Useful contribution does not always arrive with recognition. Quiet service still has real effects, though appreciation and fair credit matter too.","What helpful action could you take without turning it into a performance?"],["Build a Life You Can Inhabit","A person redesigned their schedule around health, relationships, learning, and meaningful work instead of appearances alone.","A life is not merely a portfolio for others to inspect. It should also be a place where you can live with reasonable balance and self-respect.","What one adjustment would make your daily life fit your values better?"]],"kind":"free"},
{"id":"art-of-starting-over","title":"The Art of Starting Over","palette":["#9a6750","#4a2b24"],"themes":["Renewal","Learning","Resilience"],"description":"A grounded guide to beginning after a plan changes, learning from experience, preserving what remains useful, and making a thoughtful next move.","chapters":[["An Ending Is Information","A small project closed after the team learned that demand was lower than expected. They kept the skills and records for the next attempt.","An ending can contain useful evidence without defining a person's worth. Examine what happened before deciding what it says about the future.","What did a recent ending teach you about your next decision?"],["Keep the Lesson, Not Every Burden","A traveller kept carrying broken equipment because it had been expensive, even after it no longer helped the journey.","Past investment cannot always justify future cost. Preserve what remains useful and release what prevents a better plan.","What are you keeping mainly because you already invested in it?"],["Begin With What Remains","After a move, a student had fewer resources but still had a notebook, a schedule, and one supportive contact.","A restart does not require perfect conditions. Identify available resources, limitations, and one practical first step.","List three resources you still have and one way to use them."],["Make the Next Attempt Smaller","A business owner tested a new idea with a small group before investing all available funds.","A smaller experiment can provide evidence while limiting avoidable risk. Progress can be deliberate rather than dramatic.","What low-cost test could teach you whether an idea is useful?"],["Ask for Help Without Shame","A craftsperson improved faster after asking a more experienced colleague to review a difficult step.","Help can shorten confusion and reveal blind spots. Asking clearly is a skill, not proof of incapacity.","Who could offer a useful perspective, and what specific question could you ask?"],["Let the New Chapter Be Different","A person rebuilt a routine after a major change and found that the new version suited their present responsibilities better.","Starting over need not mean recreating the past. Use earlier lessons while adapting to current reality.","What do you want to carry forward, and what should be different this time?"]],"kind":"free"}
];
const $=id=>document.getElementById(id),premiumShelf=$("premiumShelf"),freeShelf=$("freeShelf"),details=$("bookDetails"),reader=$("bookReader"),backdrop=$("bookModalBackdrop");
function closeDetails(){details.hidden=true;if(backdrop)backdrop.hidden=true;document.body.classList.remove("modal-open")}
function openDetails(){details.hidden=false;if(backdrop)backdrop.hidden=false;document.body.classList.add("modal-open");setTimeout(()=> $("closeDetails").focus(),0)}
let activeBook=null,pages=[],pageIndex=0,fontSize=18;
const esc=s=>String(s??"").replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({"&":"&amp;","<":"&lt;",">":"&gt;",'"':"&quot;","'":"&#39;"}[c]));
function spine(book,isPremium){const b=document.createElement("button");b.type="button";b.className="library-book "+(isPremium?"premium-book":"free-book");b.style.background=isPremium?"linear-gradient(155deg,#c4a25d,#60451e)":"linear-gradient(155deg,"+book.palette[0]+","+book.palette[1]+")";b.dataset.bookId=book.id;b.setAttribute("aria-label","Open details for "+book.title);b.innerHTML='<span class="book-spine-publisher">VOICEOFTAADE</span><span class="book-spine-mark">✳</span><span class="book-spine-title">'+esc(book.title)+'</span><span class="book-spine-author">'+esc(isPremium?"Taiwo A. Adekunle":"Voiceoftaade")+'</span>';b.addEventListener("click",()=>showDetails(book));return b}
premium.forEach(b=>premiumShelf.appendChild(spine(b,true)));
function renderFree(query){freeShelf.replaceChildren();const q=(query||"").toLowerCase().trim();const matches=free.filter(b=>!q||[b.title,b.description,...b.themes].join(" ").toLowerCase().includes(q));if(!matches.length){const p=document.createElement("p");p.className="shelf-no-results";p.textContent="No free titles match that search. Try love, purpose, discipline, or wisdom.";freeShelf.appendChild(p);return}for(let start=0;start<matches.length;start+=20){const row=document.createElement("div");row.className="shelf-board free-board";row.setAttribute("role","group");row.setAttribute("aria-label","Free books shelf "+(Math.floor(start/20)+1));matches.slice(start,start+20).forEach(book=>row.appendChild(spine(book,false)));freeShelf.appendChild(row)}}
renderFree("");
function showDetails(book){activeBook=book;reader.hidden=true;openDetails();$("detailLabel").textContent=book.kind==="premium"?"PREMIUM · PAID PUBLICATION":"FREE ORIGINAL BOOK";$("detailTitle").textContent=book.title;$("detailByline").textContent=(book.author||"Voiceoftaade")+" · Voiceoftaade Publications";$("detailDescription").textContent=book.description;$("detailThemes").innerHTML=book.themes.map(t=>'<span class="detail-chip">'+esc(t)+'</span>').join("");$("detailChapters").innerHTML=book.chapters.map(c=>'<li>'+esc(Array.isArray(c)?c[0]:c)+'</li>').join("");const art=$("detailArt");art.style.background=book.kind==="premium"?"linear-gradient(155deg,#c4a25d,#60451e)":"linear-gradient(155deg,"+book.palette[0]+","+book.palette[1]+")";art.querySelector("strong").textContent=book.title;const actions=$("detailActions");actions.replaceChildren();if(book.kind==="premium"){const a=document.createElement("a");a.className="button button-gold";a.href=book.buy;a.target="_blank";a.rel="noopener noreferrer";a.textContent="Purchase on Payhip ↗";actions.appendChild(a);const r=document.createElement("a");r.className="button button-outline";r.href=book.read;r.textContent="Open existing reader ↗";actions.appendChild(r);$("detailNote").textContent="This existing paid publication keeps its official Payhip purchase and reader links unchanged."}else{const r=document.createElement("button");r.className="button button-dark";r.type="button";r.textContent="Start reading · 30 sections";r.addEventListener("click",()=>startReading(book));actions.appendChild(r);const s=document.createElement("button");s.className="button button-outline";s.type="button";s.textContent="Share this book ↗";s.addEventListener("click",()=>shareBook(book));actions.appendChild(s);$("detailNote").textContent="Original free reading edition: six chapters, each with story, core idea, practical lesson, reflection questions, and an application section."} }
function buildPages(book){const out=[];book.chapters.forEach((c,i)=>{const n=i+1;out.push({title:"Chapter "+n+" · "+c[0],kind:"Story",body:c[1]});out.push({title:"Chapter "+n+" · The central idea",kind:"Reflection",body:c[2]});out.push({title:"Chapter "+n+" · Practical lesson",kind:"Practice",body:c[3]});out.push({title:"Chapter "+n+" · Questions to sit with",kind:"Reflection questions",body:"Consider the chapter in the context of your life. What feels familiar? What challenges an assumption? What detail helps you see the issue differently? You do not need a perfect answer now. Write what you know, what remains uncertain, and what you want to understand more clearly.",prompt:"What honest question has this chapter helped you ask?"});out.push({title:"Chapter "+n+" · Put the idea into practice",kind:"Action page",body:"Turn the idea into one modest action that fits ordinary circumstances. Decide when you will do it, what might make it difficult, and how you will adjust if the first attempt does not work. The aim is not to prove your worth through a perfect result; it is to learn from a deliberate attempt and return with better information.",prompt:"My next step is…"})});return out.slice(0,30)}
function startReading(book){if(book.kind==="premium")return;activeBook=book;pages=buildPages(book);pageIndex=0;fontSize=18;$("readerTitle").textContent=book.title;closeDetails();reader.hidden=false;renderPage();reader.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})}
function renderPage(){const p=pages[pageIndex];$("readerChapterTitle").textContent=p.title;$("readerPageText").innerHTML='<p class="reader-kind">'+esc(p.kind)+'</p><p>'+esc(p.body)+'</p>'+(p.prompt?'<div class="reader-prompt"><strong>Write or think about this</strong><p>'+esc(p.prompt)+'</p></div>':"");$("pageCounter").textContent="Section "+(pageIndex+1)+" of "+pages.length;$("previousPage").disabled=pageIndex===0;$("nextPage").textContent=pageIndex===pages.length-1?"Finish reading ✓":"Next →";$("readerPage").style.fontSize=fontSize+"px"}
async function shareBook(book){const url=location.href.split("#")[0]+"#"+book.id,data={title:book.title,text:"Read "+book.title+" from Voiceoftaade Publications.",url};try{if(navigator.share)await navigator.share(data);else if(navigator.clipboard){await navigator.clipboard.writeText(data.text+" "+url);alert("Book link copied. Share it with a reader.")}else prompt("Copy this book link:",url)}catch(e){if(e.name!=="AbortError")prompt("Copy this book link:",url)}}
$("shelfSearch").addEventListener("input",e=>renderFree(e.target.value));$("closeDetails").addEventListener("click",closeDetails);if(backdrop)backdrop.addEventListener("click",closeDetails);$("closeReader").addEventListener("click",()=>reader.hidden=true);document.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{if(e.key==="Escape"){if(readingModal&&!readingModal.hidden)closeReadingModal();else if(!details.hidden)closeDetails();else if(!reader.hidden)reader.hidden=true}});
$("previousPage").addEventListener("click",()=>{if(pageIndex>0){pageIndex--;renderPage();$("readerPage").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})}});
$("nextPage").addEventListener("click",()=>{if(pageIndex<pages.length-1){pageIndex++;renderPage();$("readerPage").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"})}else{reader.hidden=true;if(activeBook)showDetails(activeBook)}});
$("fontDown").addEventListener("click",()=>{fontSize=Math.max(14,fontSize-1);renderPage()});$("fontUp").addEventListener("click",()=>{fontSize=Math.min(25,fontSize+1);renderPage()});$("backToDetails").addEventListener("click",e=>{e.preventDefault();reader.hidden=true;if(activeBook)showDetails(activeBook)});
const toggle=$("menuToggle"),nav=$("mainNav");if(toggle&&nav){toggle.addEventListener("click",()=>{const open=nav.classList.toggle("open");toggle.setAttribute("aria-expanded",String(open));toggle.textContent=open?"×":"☰"});nav.querySelectorAll("a").forEach(a=>a.addEventListener("click",()=>{if(a.classList.contains("nav-dropdown-trigger"))return;nav.classList.remove("open");toggle.setAttribute("aria-expanded","false");toggle.textContent="☰"}))}
const year=$("year");if(year)year.textContent=new Date().getFullYear();
const readingBackdrop=$("readingModalBackdrop"),readingModal=$("readingModal"),readingContent=$("readingModalContent"),readingTitle=$("readingModalTitle"),closeReading=$("closeReadingModal");
function closeReadingModal(){if(readingModal)readingModal.hidden=true;if(readingBackdrop)readingBackdrop.hidden=true;document.body.classList.remove("reading-modal-open")}
function openReadingModal(id){const template=$("reading-template-"+id);if(!template||!readingModal||!readingContent)return;const article=template.content.querySelector("article");if(!article)return;readingTitle.textContent=article.querySelector("h2")?.textContent||"Full manuscript";readingContent.replaceChildren(article.cloneNode(true));readingModal.hidden=false;readingBackdrop.hidden=false;document.body.classList.add("reading-modal-open");if(closeReading)closeReading.focus()}
document.querySelectorAll("[data-reading-id]").forEach(button=>button.addEventListener("click",()=>openReadingModal(button.dataset.readingId)));
if(closeReading)closeReading.addEventListener("click",closeReadingModal);if(readingBackdrop)readingBackdrop.addEventListener("click",closeReadingModal);
const worksheet=$("downloadWorksheet");if(worksheet)worksheet.addEventListener("click",()=>{const text="VOICEOFTAADE PUBLICATIONS\nPERSONAL REFLECTION WORKSHEET\n\n1. What idea stood out to me today?\n\n____________________________\n\n2. What do I know for certain, and what am I assuming?\n\n____________________________\n\n3. What is one perspective I have not considered?\n\n____________________________\n\n4. What boundary or small promise would help me act with intention?\n\n____________________________\n\n5. What is one practical step I will take, and when?\n\n____________________________\n\nRead · Reflect · Live deliberately.\nVoiceoftaade Publications";const blob=new Blob([text],{type:"text/plain;charset=utf-8"});const url=URL.createObjectURL(blob);const a=document.createElement("a");a.href=url;a.download="Voiceoftaade-Reflection-Worksheet.txt";document.body.appendChild(a);a.click();a.remove();URL.revokeObjectURL(url)});
})();